"view.astrocycles.help"                    = "Enter the start date and the end date. 'G' stands for the standard Gregorian calendar and 'J' for the Julian calendar.\n\nSelect whether you want to work geocentrically or heliocentrically.\n\nChoose one of the coordinates. If you have selected heliocentric positions, declination and right ascension are not available.\n\nUsing the ayanamsha you can select one of the sidereal zodiacs. For the tropical zodiac use 'Tropical: No ayanamsha'.\n\nEnigma can work with single factors. You will then get a cycle of one or more celestial bodies. You can select a maximum of 12 factors.\n\nYou can also choose pairs of factors. Enigma will then show the difference for each chosen pair, measured in the selected coordinates. The maximum number of pairs is 6. Choose a factor under 'Factor A' and a factor under 'Factor B', then click 'Add pair'. You can remove a pair by clicking the cross in the 'Added pairs' list.\n\nOnce you have filled in the data, click 'Calculate'. Enigma will then automatically show a chart of the defined cycles.\n\nPlease note that there is a limit to the period you choose, depending on the selected factors. The intervals also differ.\n\nEnigma calculates the Moon 10 times per day; the period for the Moon is limited to 2,000 days (just under 5.5 years).\n\nThe Sun, Earth, Mercury, Venus, Mars, Pallas, Juno, Vesta, and the mean Black Moon are calculated once per day for a maximum period of 20,000 days (just over 54 years).\n\nJupiter, Saturn, and Ceres are calculated once every 5 days and have a maximum period of 200,000 days (just over 547 years).\n\nUranus, Neptune, and Pluto are calculated every 10 days for a maximum period of 750,000 days (2,053 years).\n\nAll other factors are also available every 10 days for a maximum period of 200,000 days.\n\nIf you select multiple factors, the shortest period applies.";
"view.astrocycles.chart.help"              = "Here you can see the chart of the cycles you have defined. The factors or factor pairs each have their own colour. You can export the chart as a PNG image using the 'Export' button.\n\nThe 'Positions' button takes you to a table with all calculated positions. Use the 'DMS' and 'Decimal' buttons to indicate whether you want to see positions in degrees, minutes and seconds or in decimal format. You can export the positions to CSV format using the 'Export' button.";
